From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timon Stipkovits <timon2201@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] x86/tsc: Don't watchdog the TSC on single-socket AMD Zen

Since commit b50db7095fe0 ("x86/tsc: Disable clocksource watchdog for
TSC on qualified platorms") the clocksource watchdog leaves the TSC
alone when the CPU has a constant, non-stop TSC and TSC_ADJUST.  AMD
added TSC_ADJUST with Zen 5, so on Zen 1-4 the watchdog keeps comparing
the TSC against the HPET or the ACPI PM timer, and Ryzen systems
regularly lose the TSC to it: "Marking clocksource 'tsc' as unstable
because the skew is too large", then the HPET for the rest of the boot.

That is expensive for games.  With the HPET every clock_gettime() falls
back from the vDSO to a syscall and an HPET read; under Wine each of
those syscalls is also caught by Syscall User Dispatch.  On a Ryzen 9
7950X3D running THE FINALS this showed up as 1.2-1.6 million trapped
syscalls a second on boots where the TSC had been rejected, against
~200k a second otherwise (CachyOS/proton-cachyos#322).

Apply the same trust to AMD and Hygon family 0x17 and later with a
constant and non-stop TSC, on a single package, outside a VM.  The TSC
synchronization check at CPU bringup still runs (it does not depend on
the watchdog), so TSCs that really disagree are still caught, and
tsc=watchdog turns the watchdog back on.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c b/arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c
index 723347e2c..b37e83bd3 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c
@@ -1245,6 +1245,26 @@ static void __init check_system_tsc_reliable(void)
 	    boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_TSC_ADJUST) &&
 	    topology_max_packages() <= 4)
 		tsc_disable_clocksource_watchdog();
+
+	/*
+	 * AMD added TSC_ADJUST with Zen 5, so the rule above never covers
+	 * Zen 1-4, and there the watchdog keeps comparing the TSC against
+	 * the HPET or the ACPI PM timer.  Under load, or with a read of the
+	 * slow watchdog clock delayed, it decides the skew is too large and
+	 * switches the whole system to the HPET for good, which makes every
+	 * clock_gettime() a syscall and an HPET read.  On a single package
+	 * with an invariant TSC, trust it like the rule above does: the TSC
+	 * synchronization check at CPU bringup still runs, and tsc=watchdog
+	 * turns the watchdog back on.
+	 */
+	if ((boot_cpu_data.x86_vendor == X86_VENDOR_AMD ||
+	     boot_cpu_data.x86_vendor == X86_VENDOR_HYGON) &&
+	    boot_cpu_data.x86 >= 0x17 &&
+	    boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_CONSTANT_TSC) &&
+	    boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_NONSTOP_TSC) &&
+	    !boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_HYPERVISOR) &&
+	    topology_max_packages() == 1)
+		tsc_disable_clocksource_watchdog();
 }
 
